2.7.15.m:
The sacred formulation born first in the east, the eager one has unveiled from the boundary of brilliant light;
he has revealed its lowest stations, its highest, the womb of the existent and of the nonexistent.
Hiraṇyagarbha came into being in the beginning; born, he was the sole lord of what has come to be.
He supported this earth and the heaven. To what god should we pay homage with the oblation?
Gathered from the waters, and from the sap of the earth, he came into being out of Viśvakarman;
Tvaṣṭṛ comes shaping his form; that was the divine birth of the Puruṣa in the beginning.
The drop has fallen.
Homage be to the serpents, whichever are along the earth,
whichever are in the intermediate space, whichever are in heaven, to those serpents homage!
Those that are arrows of the sorcerers, those of the trees,
those that lie in pits, to those serpents homage!
Those that are in the realm of light of heaven, or those in the rays of the sun,
those that have made their seats in the waters, to those serpents homage!
Assume force as if a wide net; advance like a king with his elephant, terrible!
Following your swift net, rushing, you are a shooter: pierce the demons with your hottest [arrows]!
Your whirling [flames] fly swiftly; blazing boldly, touch them!
With heats, O Agni, with your tongue, unhindered, send forth the sparks, the meteors on every side!
Send forth your scouts, be most rapid; be an unfoiled protector of this folk!
He who speaks evil against us far off, he who speaks evil near at hand, O Agni, let none of your tormentors overpower you!
Rise up, O Agni, stretch out against [them]; burn down the adversary, O you of sharp missile!
He who has practiced hostility against us, O kindled one, burn him down low like dry brushwood!
Be upright, pierce [the foe] away from us; make manifest the divine powers, O Agni!
Slacken the taut [bows] of those impelled by sorcery; crush the kin and the non-kin, the enemies!
This Agni is the lord of thousandfold, of hundredfold prize,
the head, the poet of riches.
By the heat of Agni I set you down;
with that deity, in the manner of the Aṅgirases, sit firm!
You have become the leader of the sacrifice and of the realm of space, where you accompany with auspicious teams;
in heaven you have set your head winning the sun; your tongue, O Agni, you have made the conveyer of oblations.
By the vigor of Indra I set you down;
with that deity, in the manner of the Aṅgirases, sit firm!
Firm are you, supporting,
unthrown, well held by Viśvakarman.
May the ocean not strike you up; may the well-winged bird not [strike you]!
Unwavering, make the earth firm!
You are heat;
give me heat;
sustain the earth;
make the earth firm;
do not harm the earth;
protect me from the earth!
You are light;
give me light;
sustain the intermediate space;
make the intermediate space firm;
do not harm the intermediate space;
protect me from the intermediate space!
You are heaven;
give me heaven;
sustain the sky;
make the sky firm;
do not harm the sky;
protect me from the sky!
What moist wombs are yours, O Agni, what nesting-places, what drops, O Agni, what navels are yours,
in agreement with both of these, knowing, sit down here with your body!
Sprouting from every node, from every joint,
even so, O dūrvā-grass, stretch out for us with a thousand and with a hundred!
You who stretch out with a hundred, who sprout with a thousand,
to you, O divine brick, may we pay homage with the oblation!

2.7.17.m:
Yoke, O Agni, those horses of yours that are good, god,
that, swift, bear sufficiently!
Yoke, O Agni, the horse that summons gods best, like a charioteer;
sit down as the ancient Hotṛ!
Flowing together like streams, the words of praise, purified in heart and mind;
I gaze upon the streams of ghee, the golden reed in the middle of Agni.
For the verse, thee!
For brilliance, thee!
For light, thee!
For brightness, thee!
This has become the steed of the whole world and of Agni Vaiśvānara.
Agni is full of fiery energy through fiery energy;
the gold plate is full of luster through luster.
A giver of thousands art thou;
for a thousand, thee!
Anoint the Āditya, the embryo, with milk, the all-formed counterpart of a thousand;
bypass [him] with heat, do not burn [him]; being piled, make him live a hundred years!
The rush of the wind, the navel of Varuṇa, the horse born in the middle of the flood,
the child of the rivers, the tawny one rooted in the rock—O Agni, do not harm him in the highest firmament!
The unceasing, ruddy, active drop, Agni, do I praise with homages at the early thought;
he, fitting together limb by limb according to the seasons—do not harm the cow, Aditi, the wide ruler!
The Varutrī of Tvaṣṭṛ, the navel of Varuṇa, the sheep born beyond the space,
the great, thousandfold wonder-working power of the Asura—O Agni, do not harm her in the highest firmament!
That fire which was born from Agni's heat, from his burning, over the earth or from heaven,
which Viśvakarman begot as these creatures—O Agni, let your wrath bypass him!
Do not harm this two-footed beast, thou thousand-eyed one, being piled for the offering!
The wild ape I assign unto thee;
with him piling [the altar], sit down in thy body!
Let thy burning pain reach the ape;
let thy burning pain reach him whom we hate!
Do not harm this single-hoofed beast, neighing, the courser among coursers!
The wild gaur I assign unto thee;
with him piling [the altar], sit down in thy body!
Let thy burning pain reach the gaur;
let thy burning pain reach him whom we hate!
This thousandfold, hundred-streamed fountain, opening out in the middle of the flood,
Aditi yielding ghee for the folk—O Agni, do not harm her in the highest firmament!
The wild gavaya I assign unto thee;
with him piling [the altar], sit down in thy body!
Let thy burning pain reach the gavaya;
let thy burning pain reach him whom we hate!
This woolly one, the navel of Varuṇa, the skin of beasts, two-footed and four-footed,
the first birthplace of Tvaṣṭṛ, of the gods—O Agni, do not harm him in the highest firmament!
The wild ram I assign unto thee;
with him piling [the altar], sit down in thy body!
Let thy burning pain reach the ram;
let thy burning pain reach him whom we hate!
For the he-goat was born from Agni's heat; he saw the begetter in the beginning;
by him the gods went to divinity, to the highest; by him, fit for sacrifice, they went to the heights.
The wild śarabha I assign unto thee;
with him piling [the altar], sit down in thy body!
Let thy burning pain reach the śarabha;
let thy burning pain reach him whom we hate!

2.7.19.m:
This one in front is the earth;
his breath is derived from the earth;
spring is derived from breath;
the Gāyatrī is connected with spring;
from the Gāyatrī, the Gāyatra [sāman];
from the Gāyatra, the Upāṃśu [cup];
from the Upāṃśu, the Trivṛt [stoma];
from the Trivṛt, the Rathantara [sāman];
Vasiṣṭha is the ṛṣi.
With thee, taken by Prajāpati, I take breath for the creatures.
This one on the south is Viśvakarman;
his mind is derived from Viśvakarman;
summer is derived from mind;
the Triṣṭubh is connected with summer;
from the Triṣṭubh, the Svāra [sāman];
from the Svāra, the Antaryāma [cup];
from the Antaryāma, the Pañcadaśa [stoma];
from the Pañcadaśa, the Bṛhat [sāman];
Bharadvāja is the ṛṣi.
With thee, taken by Prajāpati, I take the mind for the creatures.
This one behind is of all-encompassing range;
his eye is derived from the all-encompassing range;
the rainy season is derived from the eye;
the Jagatī is connected with the rainy season;
from the Jagatī, the Ṛk-sama [sāman];
from the Ṛk-sama, the Śukra [cup];
from the Śukra, the Saptadaśa [stoma];
from the Saptadaśa, the Vairūpa [sāman];
Jamadagni is the ṛṣi.
With thee, taken by Prajāpati, I take the eye for the creatures.
This one on the north is heaven;
his hearing is derived from heaven;
autumn is derived from hearing;
the Anuṣṭubh is connected with autumn;
from the Anuṣṭubh, the Aiḍa [sāman];
from the Aiḍa, the Manthin [cup];
from the Manthin, the Ekaviṃśa [stoma];
from the Ekaviṃśa, the Vairāja [sāman];
Viśvāmitra is the ṛṣi.
With thee, taken by Prajāpati, I take hearing for the creatures.
This one above is thought;
her speech is derived from thought;
winter is derived from speech;
the Paṅkti is connected with winter;
from the Paṅkti, the Nidhanavat [sāman];
from the Nidhanavat, the Āgrayaṇa [cup];
from the Āgrayaṇa, the Triṇava and Trayastriṃśa [stomas];
from the Triṇava and Trayastriṃśa, the Śākvara and Raivata [sāmans];
Viśvakarman is the ṛṣi.
With thee, taken by Prajāpati, I take speech for the creatures.

2.7.20.m:
The eastern direction;
the spring season;
Agni the deity;
the priesthood the wealth;
the Gāyatrī the meter;
the Rathantara the sāman;
the fifteenfold [stoma] the course;
Sānaga the ṛṣi;
the eighteen-month-old [calf] the age;
the Kṛta among throws of dice;
the east wind the wind.
The fathers, grandfathers, earlier [and] later,
may they aid us,
may they protect us in this priesthood, in this office of purohita, in this rite, in this wish, in this invocation of the gods!
The southern direction;
the summer season;
Indra the deity;
the princely power the wealth;
the Triṣṭubh the meter;
the Bṛhat the sāman;
the fifteenfold the stoma;
and it has the seventeenfold course;
Sanātana the ṛṣi;
the two-year-old [bull] the age;
the Tretā among throws of dice;
the south wind the wind.
The fathers, grandfathers, earlier [and] later,
may they aid us,
may they protect us in this priesthood, in this office of purohita, in this rite, in this wish, in this invocation of the gods!
The western direction;
the rainy season the season;
the All-Gods the deity;
the commonalty the wealth;
the Jagatī the meter;
the Vairūpa the sāman;
the seventeenfold the stoma;
and it has the twenty-onefold course;
Ahabhūna the ṛṣi;
the three-year-old [bull] the age;
the Dvāpara among throws of dice;
the west wind the wind.
The fathers, grandfathers, earlier [and] later,
may they aid us,
may they protect us in this priesthood, in this office of purohita, in this rite, in this wish, in this invocation of the gods!
The northern direction;
the autumn season;
Mitra and Varuṇa the deities;
prosperity the wealth;
the Anuṣṭubh the meter;
the Vairāja the sāman;
the twenty-onefold the stoma;
and it has the twenty-sevenfold course;
Purāṇa the ṛṣi;
the four-year-old [bull] the age;
the Abhibhava among throws of dice;
the north wind the wind.
The fathers, grandfathers, earlier [and] later,
may they aid us,
may they protect us in this priesthood, in this office of purohita, in this rite, in this wish, in this invocation of the gods!
The upward direction;
the winter and dewy seasons the two seasons;
Bṛhaspati the deity;
fruit the wealth;
the Paṅkti the meter;
the Śākvara and Raivata the two sāmans;
the twenty-sevenfold the stoma;
and it has the thirty-threefold course;
Suparṇa the ṛṣi;
the five-year-old [bull] the age;
the Āskanda among throws of dice;
the wind from above the wind.
The fathers, grandfathers, earlier [and] later,
may they aid us,
may they protect us in this priesthood, in this office of purohita, in this rite, in this wish, in this invocation of the gods!

2.8.2.m:
Protect my exhalation!
Protect my inhalation!
Protect my circulating breath!
Illuminate widely my sight!
Cause my hearing to be praised!
Swell the waters!
Quicken the plants!
Protect the two-footed!
Guard the four-footed!
Send down rain from the sky!
Ruling power is the age;
Mayanta is the meter.
Support is the age;
Adhipati is the meter.
The head is the age;
Prajāpati is the meter.
Viśvakarman is the age;
Parameṣṭhin is the meter.
The eighteen-month-old [calf] is the age;
the Triṣṭubh is the meter.
The two-year-old [bull] is the age;
the Virāj is the meter.
The thirty-month-old [calf] is the age;
the Gāyatrī is the meter.
The three-year-old [bull] is the age;
the Uṣṇih is the meter.
The four-year-old [bull] is the age;
the Anuṣṭubh is the meter.
The five-year-old [bull] is the age;
the Bṛhatī is the meter.
The breeding bull is the age;
the Kakubh is the meter.
The bull is the age;
the Satobṛhatī is the meter.
The draft-ox is the age;
the Paṅkti is the meter.
The milch-cow is the age;
the Jagatī is the meter.
The he-goat is the age;
Yuvala is the meter.
The ram is the age;
Viśāla is the meter.
Man is the age;
Tandra is the meter.
The lion is the age;
Chadis is the meter.
The tiger is the age;
Anādhṛṣya is the meter.

2.8.10.m:
This one in the east is Harikeśa, a ray of the sun;
his general and village-headman are Rathakṛtsna and Rathaujas;
Puñjikasthalā and Kṛtasthalā are his two apsarases;
sorcerers are his missile,
rākṣasas his counter-missile.
Homage be to them!
May they be gracious to us!
Whom we hate and who hates us, him we place into their jaws.
This one in the south is Viśvakarman;
his general and village-headman are Rathasvana and Rathecitra;
Menakā and Sahajanyā are his two apsarases;
biting beasts are his missile,
homicide is his counter-missile.
Homage be to them!
May they be gracious to us!
Whom we hate and who hates us, him we place into their jaws.
This one in the west is Vidadvasu;
his general and village-headman are Rathaprota and Asamaratha;
Āmlocantī and Pramlocantī are his two apsarases;
tigers are his missile,
serpents his counter-missile.
Homage be to them!
May they be gracious to us!
Whom we hate and who hates us, him we place into their jaws.
This one in the north is Saṃyadvasu;
his general and village-headman are Senajit and Suṣeṇa;
Viśvācī and Ghṛtācī are his two apsarases;
waters are his missile,
wind his counter-missile.
Homage be to them!
May they be gracious to us!
Whom we hate and who hates us, him we place into their jaws.
This one above is Arvāgvasu;
his general and village-headman are Tārkṣya and Ariṣṭanemi;
Urvaśī and Pūrvacitti are his two apsarases;
thundering from above is his missile,
lightning his counter-missile.
Homage be to them!
May they be gracious to us!
Whom we hate and who hates us, him we place into their jaws.

2.8.11.m:
With the eastern direction I set thee down.
With Agni the god as deity, with the gāyatrī meter, I lay down the head of Agni;
with the head of Agni of the gāyatrī meter I lay down the head of Agni.
With the southern direction I set thee down.
With Indra the god as deity, with the triṣṭubh meter, I lay down the wing of Agni;
with the wing of Agni of the triṣṭubh meter I lay down the wing of Agni.
With the western direction I set thee down.
With the All-Gods as deity, with the jagatī meter, I lay down the tail of Agni;
with the tail of Agni of the jagatī meter I lay down the tail of Agni.
With the northern direction I set thee down.
With Mitra and Varuṇa, the two gods, as deity, with the anuṣṭubh meter, I lay down the wing of Agni;
with the wing of Agni of the anuṣṭubh meter I lay down the wing of Agni.
With the upward direction I set thee down.
With Bṛhaspati the god as deity, with the paṅkti meter, I lay down the back of Agni;
with the back of Agni of the paṅkti meter I lay down the back of Agni.

2.8.12.m:
Madhu and Mādhava, the two spring seasons: / thou art the inner clamp of Agni.
May heaven and earth be shaped [for me]; may the waters and plants be shaped [for me]; / may the fires separately, sharing my vow, be shaped for my preeminence.
The fires that are like-minded between heaven and earth, / adapting themselves to the two spring seasons, may they assemble together around [me] as the gods around Indra.
Śukra and Śuci, the two summer seasons;
Nabhas and Nabhasya, the two rainy seasons;
Iṣ and Ūrj, the two autumn seasons;
Sahas and Sahasya, the two winter seasons;
Tapas and Tapasya, the two cool seasons: / thou art the inner clamp of Agni.
May heaven and earth be shaped [for me]; may the waters and plants be shaped [for me]; / may the fires separately, sharing my vow, be shaped for my preeminence.
The fires that are like-minded between heaven and earth, / adapting themselves to the two cool seasons, may they assemble together around [me] as the gods around Indra.

2.8.13.m:
Thou art the winner of the east wind.
Thou art the winner of cloud.
Thou art the winner of lightning.
Thou art the winner of thunder.
Thou art the winner of rain.
Thou art the vehicle of Agni.
Thou art the vehicle to Agni of Agni.
Thou art the vehicle of Vāyu.
Thou art the vehicle to Vāyu of Vāyu.
Thou art the vehicle of the gods.
Thou art the vehicle to the gods of the gods.
Thou art the vehicle of the All-Gods.
Thou art the vehicle to the gods of the All-Gods.
Thou art the converging vehicle.
Thou art sitting in the atmosphere.
Sit in the atmosphere.
Ambā and Bulā and Nitatnī and Stanayantī and Abhrayantī and Meghayantī and Cupuṇīkā:
for the flood [I set] thee,
for grace thee,
for truth thee,
for intent thee,
for good intent thee,
for like intent thee,
for Vivasvat thee,
for the sky, for light thee,
for the Ādityas thee.

2.8.14.m:
Water-fortress art thou by name, pervaded with food.
Unto thee I approach with myself, with men, with cows, with horses, with life, with splendor, with offspring, with wealth, with gain, with wisdom, with riches, with thriving.
Men are thy guardians;
Agni is the sovereign lord.
With that deity, like the Aṅgirases, sit firm.
Unconquered art thou by name, pervaded with the Brahman.
Unto thee I approach with myself, with men, with cows, with horses, with life, with splendor, with offspring, with wealth, with gain, with wisdom, with riches, with thriving.
The Maruts are thy guardians;
Vāyu is the sovereign lord.
With that deity, like the Aṅgirases, sit firm.
Over-heaven art thou by name, pervaded with immortality.
Unto thee I approach with myself, with men, with cows, with horses, with life, with splendor, with offspring, with wealth, with gain, with wisdom, with riches, with thriving.
The All-Gods are thy guardians;
Sūrya is the sovereign lord.
With that deity, like the Aṅgirases, sit firm.
May Prajāpati seat thee on the back of the earth.
Earth art thou;
ground art thou;
breadth art thou;
wide earth art thou.
Aditi art thou, all-sustaining, supporter of the whole world.
Support the earth;
make the earth firm;
injure not the earth.
Protect me from the earth for all out-breathing, in-breathing, inter-breathing, up-breathing, for standing firm, for moving about.
May Agni protect thee all around with great well-being, with most auspicious protection.
May Viśvakarman seat thee on the back of the atmosphere.
Support the atmosphere;
make the atmosphere firm;
injure not the atmosphere.
Protect me from the atmosphere for all out-breathing, in-breathing, inter-breathing, up-breathing, for standing firm, for moving about.
May Vāyu protect thee all around with great well-being, with most auspicious protection.
May Parameṣṭhin seat thee on the back of heaven, spacious, broad, shining, radiant,
who shinest unto heaven, unto earth, unto the wide atmosphere.
Support heaven;
make heaven firm;
injure not heaven.
Protect me from heaven for all out-breathing, in-breathing, inter-breathing, up-breathing, for standing firm, for moving about.
May Sūrya protect thee all around with great well-being, with most auspicious protection.
Snorting like a horse desiring grass, when he stood out from the great enclosure, / then the wind blows after his flame; then may thy path be black.
In the seat of Āyu I set thee down, in the surge of the ocean, in the shade of the well.
Homage to the ocean;
homage to the eye of the ocean.
The measure of a thousand art thou;
the fore-measure of a thousand art thou;
the counterpart of a thousand art thou;
the commensurate measure of a thousand art thou;
the up-measure of a thousand art thou;
belonging to a thousand art thou;
for a thousand thee.
May these bricks be milch-cows for me, O Agni:
one and a hundred,
a hundred and a thousand,
a thousand and ten thousand,
ten thousand and a hundred thousand,
a hundred thousand and ten thousand […],
ten million and a hundred million,
a billion and a ten-billion,
a hundred-billion and a trillion.
May these bricks be milch-cows for me, O Agni, sixty, a thousand, ten thousand, unexhausted,
standing in the seasons, thriving in ṛta, dropping ghee, dropping honey, rich in sap, rich in milk, self-nourishing, nesting.
May those bricks be milch-cows for me, O Agni, Virājs by name, yielding desires yonder in that world.

2.9.9.m:
Homage to the mountain-wanderers, the hearts of the gods!
Homage to the discriminators!
Homage to the destroyers!
Homage to the crushers!
O prowler, lord of nourishment, impoverished one, blue-and-red one,
do not terrify these cattle, these offspring, do not injure them, and let none of ours be sick!
These thoughts we bring forward to the strong Rudra, the braided-haired one, who rules over heroes,
so that there may be welfare for our two-footed and four-footed, and that everything in this village may be thriving and free from illness.
That body of yours, O Rudra, which is auspicious, auspicious, having all-healing medicines,
auspicious, having medicines for affliction, with that make us live happily!
May the missile of Rudra bypass us, may the disfavor of the furious, malevolent one bypass [us];
loosen [your bowstrings] firm against our patrons; O bounteous one, have mercy on our children and offspring!
O most bounteous, most auspicious one, be auspicious to us, be well-disposed;
unstringing your bow, without anger, be well-disposed;
bearing the pināka, come hither; clothed in a hide, approach!
O diverter, bright-red one, homage be to you, O lord;
those missiles of yours that are a thousand, let them strike down others than us!
Thousands by thousands are the missiles in your arms;
as ruler of them, O generous one, turn their points away!
The innumerable thousands of Rudras who are upon the earth,
their bows we unstring a thousand leagues away.
The Bhavas who are over this great flood, the atmosphere,
their bows we unstring a thousand leagues away.
The Rudras, blue-necked, white-throated, who have resorted to heaven,
their bows we unstring a thousand leagues away.
The Śarvas, blue-necked, white-throated, who move upon the earth below,
their bows we unstring a thousand leagues away.
Those who in the trees are meadow-yellow, blue-necked, bright-red,
their bows we unstring a thousand leagues away.
Those who are overlords of beings, crestless and braided-haired,
their bows we unstring a thousand leagues away.
Those who are path-protectors of paths, bringing delight in food, your warriors,
their bows we unstring a thousand leagues away.
Those who frequent the fords, bearing darts and quivers,
their bows we unstring a thousand leagues away.
Those who pierce among the foods, [among] people drinking from vessels,
their bows we unstring a thousand leagues away.
The Rudras who, so many or more, have settled in the quarters,
their bows we unstring a thousand leagues away.
Homage be to the Rudras who are in heaven, whose arrows are rain;
to them ten [fingers] eastward, ten southward, ten westward, ten northward, ten upward;
to them be homage;
may they have mercy on us;
whomever we hate and whoever hates us, him we put into their jaws!
Homage be to the Rudras who are in the atmosphere, whose arrows are wind;
to them ten [fingers] eastward, ten southward, ten westward, ten northward, ten upward;
to them be homage;
may they have mercy on us;
whomever we hate and whoever hates us, him we put into their jaws!
Homage be to the Rudras who are on earth, whose arrows are food;
to them ten [fingers] eastward, ten southward, ten westward, ten northward, ten upward;
to them be homage;
may they have mercy on us;
whomever we hate and whoever hates us, him we put into their jaws!

2.10.3.m:
Father of the eye, for he is wise in mind, generated these two bowing ones as ghee;
when their ancient ends were fixed firm, only then did heaven and earth spread out.
Viśvakarman measured out, he who is vast, the supporter, the distributor, and the highest vision;
their offerings rejoice together with food, where they call the one beyond the seven ṛṣis.
They sacrificed wealth together into this, the ancient ṛṣis, like singers in abundance,
seated in the unseen and seen realm, who formed these created beings.
Who is our father, the begetter, who the distributor, who produced existence beyond existence for us,
who alone is the name-giver of the gods, to him other worlds go asking questions.
Beyond heaven, beyond this earth, beyond the gods, what asura-like [entity] is there?
Which first embryo did the waters hold, wherein all the gods came together?
The waters held that very first embryo, wherein all the gods saw one another;
placed on the navel of the Unborn is the One, wherein all beings rested.
If Viśvakarman, the god, was born, then the Gandharva became the second;
the third, the father, the begetter of plants; they distributed the embryo of the waters in many places.
You do not know him who generated these; something else has become intermediate between you;
shrouded in mist and with chatter, breathing-to-satisfy, reciters of ukthas, they wander about.

2.10.6.m:
Stride up to the firmament with the fire, bearing the cauldron in your hands;
having gone to the back of heaven, to the sun, sit mingled with the gods!
Along the eastern [direction] […].
Go forth, O Agni, foremost of those who seek the gods, the eye of gods and of mortals;
eager to sacrifice, along with the Bhṛgus, let the sacrificers go safely to the sun!
From the earth I have mounted up to the atmosphere; from the atmosphere I have mounted up to heaven;
from the back of the firmament of heaven I have gone to the light, to the sun.
Going to the sun, they do not look back; they mount up to heaven, to both worlds,
they who, well-knowing, have extended the sacrifice flowing in every direction.
Night and dawn […].
O Agni of a thousand eyes […].
You are the eagle, winged;
sit upon the back of the earth!
Fill the atmosphere with brightness;
prop up heaven with light;
firm the directions with splendor!
Invoked, fair-faced, in front, O Agni, sit upon your own womb, the Sādhya;
in this upper place of meeting, sit, all gods and the sacrificer!
That wondrous, all-peopled good favor of Savitṛ the desirable I choose,
which Kaṇva milked for him, the swelling, thousand-streaming, great cow with milk.
May we worship you at your highest birth, O Agni; may we worship with praises in the lower place of meeting;
from which womb you have arisen, sacrifice to that; forth into you, when kindled, have they offered the oblations.
Kindled forth, O Agni, shine before us with unceasing flame, O youngest one;
to him ever come the spoils.
"O Agni, him today" […].
"Seven are your [kindling-sticks], O Agni" […].
I offer the layer with the mind, so that the gods may come here,
the invited sacrificers, increasers of truth.
In the flight of your path, the ocean, I offer to Viśvakarman
an unassailable oblation forever.

2.11.1.m:
Bright-light, and Clear-light,
and True-light, and Luminous,
and True, and Protector of truth, and Transcending distress;
and Winner of truth, and Winner of reality,
and Winner of armies, and Having good armies;
and Having friends near, and Having foes afar, a troop;
and Cosmic Order, and Truth,
and Firm, and Supporter,
and Sustainer, and Disposer, and Maintainer;
and Such, and Such-like,
and Equal, and Counter-equal,
and Measured, and Commensurate, all-bringing ones;
such ones, such-like ones, come well to us,
equal ones, counter-equal ones, come,
measured and commensurate, to our aid, all-bringing Maruts, to this sacrifice!
The Maruts, the divine clans, follow Indra;
just as the Maruts, the divine clans, followed Indra, so may both the divine and human clans follow this sacrificer!

2.11.6.m:
And fire and the heat-cauldron;
and the sun-hymn and the sun;
and breath and the horse sacrifice;
and Aditi and the earth;
and Diti and the sky;
may the Śakvarī meters, the fingers, and the quarters be prepared for me through sacrifice!
And the season and the vow;
and the year and heat;
may day and night, the two thigh-bones, and the Bṛhat and Rathantara [chants] be prepared for me through sacrifice!
And one, and three, up to thirty-three;
and four, and eight, up to forty-eight;
and the eighteen-month-old calf, and the eighteen-month-old heifer;
and the two-year-old bull, and the two-year-old cow;
and the thirty-month-old bull, and the thirty-month-old cow;
and the three-year-old bull, and the three-year-old cow;
and the four-year-old bull, and the four-year-old cow;
and the five-year-old bull, and the five-year-old cow;
and the breeding bull, and the barren cow;
and the bull, and the miscarrying cow;
and the milch cow, and the draft ox;
and the stoma, and the yajus formula;
and the ṛk, and the sāman;
and the consecration, and heat;
and the Bṛhat, and the Rathantara;
may the herbs, the lords of the forest, and the quarters be prepared for me through sacrifice!
For food, you!
For prize-winning, you!
For winning the prize, you!
For refreshment, you!
For strength, you!
For wealth, you!
For nourishment, you!

2.12.1.m:
May all the Maruts today with all their help, may all the fires be kindled for us;
may all the gods come hither with aid, may all wealth, [all] prize be with us.
Prize is my seven directions, or the four distant realms;
may prize with all the gods aid me here in the winning of wealth.
Prize in front and from the middle for us; prize shall adapt the gods through the seasons.
For in the impulse of the prize I bend down; may I become master of the prize in every quarter.
May prize impel a gift for me today; may prize increase the gods with oblation.
For prize has made me rich in all heroes; may I, as master of the prize, win all quarters.
I unite myself with the milk of the earth, I unite myself with waters, with herbs;
so may I win the prize, O Agni.
Put milk into the earth, milk into the herbs, milk into the sky, milk into the atmosphere;
may the directions be full of milk for me.

2.12.2.m:
Conquering by truth, truth-aboded Agni is the Gandharva;
his Apsarases are the herbs, named Joys;
may he protect this our priesthood and nobility;
may they protect this our priesthood and nobility;
to him svāhā, vaṭ!
To them svāhā, vaṭ!
The gathered, all-harmonious Sun is the Gandharva;
his Apsarases are the rays, named Active;
Suṣumṇa, sun-beamed Soma the moon is the Gandharva;
his Apsarases are the lunar mansions, named Bekuris;
the vigorous, all-encompassing Wind is the Gandharva;
his Apsarases are the waters, named Strengths;
the nourishing eagle, the Sacrifice is the Gandharva;
his Apsarases are the sacrificial gifts, named Desires;
Bṛhaspati, all-working Indra is the Gandharva;
his Apsarases are the Maruts, named Might;
Prajāpati, the highest, the Mind is the Gandharva;
his Apsarases are the ṛks and sāmans, named Praises;
unpitying, distant-missiled Death is the Gandharva;
his Apsarases are the creatures, named Timid;
O Lord of the world, whose houses are above, O Lord of Virāj, grant great protection to this priesthood, to this nobility;
whose Apsarases are all the quarters, named Pleasing—may he protect this our priesthood and nobility;
may they protect this our priesthood and nobility;
to him svāhā, vaṭ!
To them svāhā, vaṭ!

2.12.3.m:
You are the ocean, vaporous, wet with drops;
bringing peace, bringing pleasure, blow unto me, svāhā!
You are aid-seeking, reverent;
bringing peace, bringing pleasure, blow unto me, svāhā!
You are of the Maruts, the host of the Maruts;
bringing peace, bringing pleasure, blow unto me, svāhā!
In union with the year through the second halves of the months,
in union with the Dawn through her ruddy ones,
in full union with the Aśvins through wondrous deeds,
in union with the Sun through Etaśa,
in union with Vaiśvānara through the offering-food, through ghee, svāhā!
I yoke Agni with might, with ghee, the heavenly eagle, large in wingspan;
with him may we fly to the summit of the ruddy one, ascending to heaven in the highest firmament.
These two wings of yours are unaging, winged, with which you strike away the demons, O Agni;
with them may we fly to the world of the righteous, where went the first, the ancient seers.
The drop, the skilled one, the falcon keeping the order, golden-winged, the bustling bird,
great in his seat, firmly seated—
homage be to you,
do not harm me!
You are the head of the sky, the navel of the earth, the strength of the waters, of the herbs;
all-living, far-reaching protection; homage to the path; you dwell established on the head of the universe.
In the ocean is your heart, life within; give the waters, cleave the water-reservoir;
from the sky, from Parjanya, from the atmosphere, from the earth—from there aid us with rain!
I unfasten your halter, I unfasten your reins, I unfasten the harness straps that go around;
grant us here auspicious wealth, declare me as bestowing a portion among the deities.
Sacrificed is the sacrifice by the Bhṛgus as giver of wealth, by the Yatis as giver of blessings, by the Vasus;
may Agni, sacrificed and invoked, fulfill our offered oblation;
blessed be this homage to the gods!

2.12.4.m:
With which austerity the ṛṣis sat at the sacrificial session, kindling Agni, bringing heaven;
which [Agni] men call the one with strewn sacred grass—on that firmament I deposit Agni.
May we follow him with wives, O gods, with sons, with brothers, or with gold,
seizing the firmament in the world of the well-done, on the third back, upon the radiance of heaven.
The bustling, perceptive Agni, the lord of the good, has mounted into the middle of speech;
laid down on the back of the earth, shining brightly, let him tread underfoot those who are hostile.
May this Agni, most heroic, bestower of vigor, belonging to the thousand, shine unheeding;
shining forth from the midst of the water, go forth unto the divine abodes!
Agni, move, go forth along together; make visible the paths that lead to the gods;
take hold of each well-done deed, where the ṛṣis have gone, who were the first, the ancient ones.
Move forth together, go forth together; make visible the paths that lead to the gods;
by these well-done deeds may we follow, O gods, where our ancient fathers have passed away.
Awake, O Agni, be watchful toward him; may you and he unite with what is sacrificed and bestowed;
making them young again, the fathers have woven along this thread of yours.
With that wherewith you bear a thousand, with that, O Agni, wherewith all property,
with that bear this our sacrifice to heaven, to go among the gods.
This is your—

2.12.5.m:
May the seasons, the years increase you, O Agni, the ṛṣis, whatever things are true;
shine forth with divine radiance; illuminate all the four quarters.
Be kindled together, O Agni, and awake him; stand upright for great good fortune;
and may not your attendant come to harm, O Agni; may your priests be glorious, not others.
You, O Agni, these Brahmins choose; be gracious to us, O Agni, in the enclosure;
be a slayer of rivals, O Agni, conqueror of enemies; watch without slumbering in your own home.
Here indeed, O Agni, uphold wealth; let not the subjugators subjugate you at the first thought;
let the nobility be easily guided for you, O Agni; may your attendant increase unvanquished.
With your own nobility, O Agni, seize [power]; with Mitra, O Agni, strive in friendship;
for standing in the midst of fellow-born, of kings, O Agni, shine here worthy of invocation.
Beyond the scoffers, beyond the slights, beyond ignorance, beyond destruction today,
for do you cross over all perils, O Agni; then grant to us wealth with heroes.
Inviolable Jātavedas, unvanquished, ruling widely, O Agni, upholding nobility, shine here;
releasing the ailments of mortal men, guard us today with gracious [aids] for our increase.
O Bṛhaspati, O Savitṛ, awaken him; sharpen him yet further, though already sharpened;
increase him for great good fortune, and let all the gods rejoice with him.
From the afterlife, from the curse of Yama, which you released [him from], O Bṛhaspati,
may the two Aśvins, the physicians of the gods, avert death from him by their powers, O Agni.
Beholding the higher light beyond the darkness,
we have gone to the god, the sun among the gods, the highest light.

2.13.11.m:
Sought in heaven, sought on earth, sought has Agni entered all plants;
Agni Vaiśvānara, sought by force, let him protect us by day, let him protect us from harm by night.
O youngest, protect the men of the giver; hear our songs;
guard the offspring, and [us] in person.
O Agni Jātavedas, god, self-willed one, your forms, the name of the immortal,
and the many wondrous powers of the craft-masters which they placed in you, O all-penetrating one, of tied back!

2.13.14.m:
The measure is the meter;
that is the earth;
Agni is the deity;
with that meter, with that sacred word, with that deity, sit firm in the manner of Aṅgiras!
The fore-measure is the meter;
that is the atmosphere;
Vāyu is the deity;
the counterpart is the meter;
that is the sky;
Sūrya is the deity;
the Asrīvī is the meter;
that is the quarters;
Soma is the deity;
the Gāyatrī is the meter;
that is the she-goat;
Bṛhaspati is the deity;
the Triṣṭubh is the meter;
that is the gold;
Indra is the deity;
the Jagatī is the meter;
that is the cow;
Prajāpati is the deity;
the Anuṣṭubh is the meter;
that is life-span;
Mitra is the deity;
the Uṣṇih is the meter;
that is the eye;
Pūṣan is the deity;
the Virāj is the meter;
that is the horse;
Varuṇa is the deity;
the Bṛhatī is the meter;
that is agriculture;
Parjanya is the deity;
the Paṅkti is the meter;
that is man;
Parameṣṭhin is the deity;
with that meter, with that sacred word, with that deity, sit firm in the manner of Aṅgiras!

2.13.15.m:
You are the earth, a barren cow by birth;
you held Agni as embryo;
come into being with me!
You are the atmosphere, a barren cow by birth;
you held Vāyu as embryo;
come into being with me!
You are the sky, a barren cow by birth;
you held the Āditya as embryo;
come into being with me!
You are the lunar mansions, a barren cow by birth;
you held the moon as embryo;
come into being with me!
You are the Ṛg, a barren cow by birth;
you held the Sāman as embryo;
come into being with me!
You are the commonalty, a barren cow by birth;
you held the king as embryo;
come into being with me!
You are speech, a barren cow by birth;
you held breath as embryo;
come into being with me!
You are the waters, a barren cow by birth;
you held sacrifice as embryo;
come into being with me!

2.13.18.m:
You are the creator of abundance;
you are the creator of wide room;
you are facing east;
you are facing upward;
you are the atmosphere.
I seat you, the giver of strength, in strength.
I seat you, the giver of milk, in milk.
I seat you, the giver of fiery brilliance, in fiery brilliance.
I seat you, the giver of fame, in fame.
I seat you, the giver of luster, in luster.
I seat you in the wealth of the earth.
I seat you in the wealth of the atmosphere.
I seat you in the wealth of the sky.
I seat you in the wealth of the quarters.
I seat you, the giver of wealth, in wealth.
You are seated in the waters;
you are seated in the vulture;
you are seated in the falcon;
you are seated in the eagle;
you are seated in the vault of heaven.
